# Every entry here is a build break, a correctness risk, or weight that slows the context
# upload. The first two are not hygiene — they change whether the image builds and what it runs.

# THE BUILD BREAK. `COPY MoneyManager.Api/ MoneyManager.Api/` would land the host's
# obj/project.assets.json on top of the one `dotnet restore` just wrote in the same layer. That
# file records absolute package paths from whatever machine last built, so the image build then
# fails with NETSDK1064 for packages that are sitting right there. It is the most common
# multi-stage .NET Dockerfile failure and it only appears when building from a used worktree.
**/obj/
**/bin/

# THE CONFIGURATION OVERRIDE. Program.cs calls DotEnv.Load() before the configuration builder
# exists, and dotenv.net overwrites already-set variables by default. A .env baked into the
# image would silently take precedence over the platform's own environment — including
# JwtSettings__SecretKey and ConnectionStrings__Default — while the dashboard went on showing
# the values it thought were in effect. .env.example documents copying one into
# MoneyManager.Api/, so this needs to match at any depth.
#
# Note the committed .env.development and .env.production are deliberately NOT matched here:
# the Vite build reads .env.production to point the SPA at the relative /api.
**/.env
**/.env.local
**/.env.*.local

# node_modules destroys the npm ci layer cache, and a host tsbuildinfo inside it can make
# `vue-tsc -b` skip work it should have done.
**/node_modules/

# A local database must never become the image's starting data. *.db does not match SQLite's
# sidecar files, so they are listed in their own right.
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# Build output and reports. dist/ and coverage/ both exist in a working tree that has run the
# UI build or the coverage suite.
**/dist/
**/coverage/
**/test-results/
**/playwright-report/

.git/
.github/
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
